### What is a prime number?

A prime number is an integer greater than one that has no positive divisors other than one and itself. For example, 7 is prime because no number from 2 to 6 divides it evenly. 9 is not prime because 3 divides it.

### Why check only up to the square root?

Divisors come in pairs that multiply to N. For 36, the pairs are (1,36), (2,18), (3,12), (4,9), (6,6). In every pair, one member is at most sqrt(N) and the other is at least sqrt(N). So if no divisor exists up to sqrt(N), none exists above it either. This reduces the work from O(n) to O(sqrt(n)).

### How do you implement it in Java?

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PrimeChecker {

    public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n < 2) return false;
        if (n == 2) return true;         // only even prime
        if (n % 2 == 0) return false;    // eliminate all other evens
        for (int i = 3; i * i <= n; i += 2) {
            if (n % i == 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Validate secure token candidates for National Bank of India
        int[] candidates = {2, 3, 4, 7, 9, 7919, 10000};
        for (int c : candidates) {
            System.out.println(c + " -> " + (isPrime(c) ? "PRIME" : "NOT PRIME"));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
2 -> PRIME
3 -> PRIME
4 -> NOT PRIME
7 -> PRIME
9 -> NOT PRIME
7919 -> PRIME
10000 -> NOT PRIME
```

### How is i * i <= n different from i <= Math.sqrt(n)?

| Feature | `i * i <= n` | `i <= Math.sqrt(n)` |
|---|---|---|
| Type | integer arithmetic | floating-point |
| Rounding risk | none | sqrt can round, missing edge |
| Overflow risk | yes, for large n | no |
| Performance | fast integer multiply | slower sqrt per iteration |

For most values `i * i <= n` is preferred. For N near `Integer.MAX_VALUE`, use `i <= n / i` to avoid overflow.

```java
// Overflow-safe bound for large n
for (int i = 3; i <= n / i; i += 2) {
    if (n % i == 0) return false;
}
```

### What are edge cases?

- 0 and 1 are not prime by definition.
- 2 is the smallest and only even prime.
- Negative numbers are not prime.

```java
System.out.println(isPrime(0));  // false
System.out.println(isPrime(1));  // false
System.out.println(isPrime(2));  // true
System.out.println(isPrime(-7)); // false
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// Wrong: loop starts at 1 (1 divides everything)
for (int i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) {
    if (n % i == 0) return false; // always returns false!
}

// Wrong: loop goes to n instead of sqrt(n)
for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) { // works but O(n), way too slow
    if (n % i == 0) return false;
}

// Correct: starts at 2, stops at sqrt(n)
for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
    if (n % i == 0) return false;
}
```

### What about large primes and cryptography?

Trial division is fine for moderate numbers, but RSA keys use primes with hundreds of digits. For those, probabilistic tests like Miller-Rabin decide primality quickly with a negligible error chance. Java provides `BigInteger.isProbablePrime(certainty)` which runs Miller-Rabin internally.

```java
import java.math.BigInteger;

BigInteger large = new BigInteger("104729");
System.out.println(large.isProbablePrime(20)); // true with high certainty
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that primality testing connects a simple loop to real financial security. The RSA keys that secure banking communications at National Bank of India depend on the difficulty of factoring the product of two large primes. Trial division checks small configuration parameters, but `BigInteger.isProbablePrime` handles cryptographic sizes.

For generating all primes up to N, the Sieve of Eratosthenes is far more efficient than testing each number individually.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you generate all primes up to N? (Sieve of Eratosthenes)
- What is the time complexity of trial division versus Miller-Rabin?
- Can you prove that divisors come in pairs below and above the square root?
